# the ELF image writer: an executable or a shared library from the image
# record of a link
#
# every address, file offset and size comes from the layout record, and every
# byte of an output section from the relocated record, so the writer computes
# no address. it builds the ELF model the ELF writer turns into bytes. the
# header takes its machine, class and byte order from the architecture row,
# its e_flags, EI_OSABI and EI_ABIVERSION from the settled facts and traits,
# with the system row's OSABI where no input states one, its type from
# whether the loader may place the image anywhere, and its entry from the
# address the link resolved for it. an image a loader binds libraries into
# holds the dynamic tables, each in the output section layout gave it, with
# PT_PHDR over the program headers, PT_INTERP over the program loader's path
# and PT_DYNAMIC over the dynamic section. a PT_LOAD maps each segment of the
# layout, its p_paddr where the layout loads its bytes, PT_TLS the thread-local block, PT_NOTE each run of loaded notes,
# PT_GNU_RELRO the run of sections read only once relocated and the
# thread-local data, PT_GNU_PROPERTY the GNU property note, PT_GNU_EH_FRAME
# the table of the image's FDEs, the vendor's program header its build
# attributes, and PT_GNU_STACK the stack, which is executable only when an
# input's stack section asks for it. the image records no stack size: the
# main thread's stack is the system's, which a size in PT_GNU_STACK does not
# reach, so a stated one is refused. the section
# headers list the output sections in image order, then the symbol table and
# the string tables. a link that needs what the image cannot hold yet is refused,
# naming what it needs. an image no loader binds holds no loader relocations.
# a stated size bounds the loaded bytes alone, which layout holds within it,
# and pads nothing: the gABI's loader maps an image by its PT_LOAD segments,
# so no byte outside them loads and a fill, which only the flat image's
# padding and gaps take, is refused.
#
# the GNU property note, the build attributes and the identification strings
# are sections the image makes from the settled record: its declarer sizes
# them before layout, and the writer fills the places layout gave them with
# the same bytes. the table of the image's FDEs is sized by what collection
# kept and written once the image is relocated, since it reads the frames
# there, so an image whose kept frames cannot all be listed is refused.

